But… If that’s the case, I feel like we might be able to simplify the process by creating an equation that takes input in the form of a Gödel number, and calculates the Gödel number of the derivational solution. Essentially, turning Gödel numbers into a lookup table for derivativations.

﻿

Your instinct is pointing toward a real idea in logic and computability, but the details matter quite a bit.

﻿

First, what the Whirligig is implicitly doing.

From what you described earlier, the device measures something like derivation difficulty and then reconstructs the derivation itself from that measure. In formal logic terms, that resembles a mapping

﻿

D \;\rightarrow\; \text{proof sequence}

﻿

where D is some scalar or structured measure of complexity. Reconstructing a proof from a compressed description of its difficulty or structure is not a standard operation in proof theory. Mathematicians usually go the other direction:

﻿

\text{proof} \;\rightarrow\; \text{complexity measure}

﻿

So if your reconstruction procedure works, it is indeed unusual. It resembles ideas from:

	•	proof enumeration

	•	Kolmogorov complexity

	•	automated theorem proving

	•	reverse search on proof graphs

﻿

Now to your Gödel-number proposal.

﻿

Gödel numbering already gives us a canonical encoding:

﻿

n = \mathrm{Gödel}(\text{formula or proof})

﻿

Every statement, symbol sequence, and proof can be encoded as an integer. Because of this, there already exists a computable function that checks whether a number encodes a valid proof of another number:

﻿

\text{Proof}(p,q)

﻿

meaning “p is the Gödel number of a proof of statement q.”

﻿

However, the function you are proposing is stronger. You are suggesting something like

﻿

f(n) = m

﻿

where

	•	n = Gödel number of a problem or theorem

	•	m = Gödel number of its derivation.

﻿

If such a function existed in general, it would effectively solve theorem proving algorithmically. For sufficiently strong systems (like arithmetic), that runs into classical limits:

﻿

• the Halting Problem

• Gödel incompleteness

• undecidability of first-order logic validity.

